I'd like to welcome our special guests  for named Murphy principal analyst for  security and risk at Forrester and Ben  McCormick vice president of operations  that Evernote Rene can you tell us a  little bit about your work at Forrester  on security in the enterprise and how  cloud figures into that yeah so I work  at Forrester as a principal analyst in  governance risk and compliance so I work  with chief information security officers  on the risk question about the cloud and  from a security perspective I'll say  that that's usually the very first  question they have how secure is the  cloud are they gonna be responsible for  you know my problems the first thing you  try to do is tell them you know listen  you know take any cloud best practice go  into your own data center and see if  you'll pass that and they'll come back  and tell me there's no way we can do  this I'm like right well then not much  left to talk about me is like someone  else can do a better so I think that's  how they they first come to the question  like what am I really giving up and I  say you're not giving up anything except  you know the spinning disk in your space  that's all you're giving up you're  actually enhancing your security you're  enhancing your availability  what makes cloud more effective is you  have the you can hire people I can't  hire you can have you know huge data  centers maybe being all of googles oh  okay  um you can do that right you can do it  in a way I can't and listen the more of  us that go to the cloud the better  you're gonna get at it and the better  you get at it the better I'm gonna be at  it and I think at the end of the day you  know if you're in the business of doing  anything other than managing data  centers then why are you managing the  data center then let me bring you in  last year you did migrate your data  centers over to Google cloud tell us a  little bit about that experience does  the security side of that mirror what  renee is talking about or did you have  other interests and concerns I'd say  broadly yes for us it was about allowing  our teams to focus on what was important  to our end customers we've got hundreds  of millions of users who we are helping  collect information and retrieve  information and help them be more  efficient being an expert in running a  physical data center doesn't directly  report that so for us it was about  allowing our technical teams and you're  right you you move to cloud you do not  get rid of your technical team yeah or  if you do you're gonna realize it's a  mistake really quickly  there are there are things you're doing  the physical data center that will be  replaced in the cloud you need to become  an expert at the clouds the cloud  providers control plane etc etc but for  us it was about focusing our company and  our staff on things that were important  to our users and also allowing our  development teams and software engineers  to move a lot faster so for us the rate  of change in the rate of innovation in  the cloud space is way fast and we can  ever achieve that's the proposition in  cloud that it automates a lot of the  rough work you don't pull cable anymore  you don't destroy hard drive anymore you  know you you outsource all that to  another provider in a large centralized  area and developers can move faster they  have better tools they can scale  ultimately security questions are about  having your business relationships  threatened and you must have had  security questions going in what were  they like and how were they best  addressed so for us to I think it was  twofold we initially started with our  standard security questionnaires and we  went through those and then what our  security team actually did was paused a  couple of months in and actually started  risk modeling or threat modeling what  were the risks that were we looking at  in cloud the risk model is not is the  cloud provider provider going to lose a  hard drive the risk model is what  happens if you lose your effectively  your root password in the cloud account  in your cloud account so we we went  through our standard process first we  learnt from that in the first cycle and  then we updated our threat modeling and  then we ask the questions to Google and  reengaged and I think the other thing we  just went through a period of learning  that you have to to go to cloud means  you've got to trust somebody is there  some kind of corollary where security is  concerned are the security teams working  in different ways on more valuable stuff  it's broadly exactly the same so the  security team and that the legal teams  have still got to verify all of the  certifications with the provider you've  got to go through this security  certifications the questionnaire  none of that changes but what the  security team is not having to do is  audit and we use this example physical  hard drive destruction they're not  having to audit physical hard drive  destruction logs every quarter and for  us the question becomes if they're not  doing that what can they be spending  their time on they can spend more time  on securing the application more time  helping our developers write more secure  code and more time chasing people that  are trying to hack our application not  trying to hack our physical data centers  so for us it I think in the security  side as well it allows them to move up  the stack and focus on things that are  more important to our customers and also  mean that that's what it's about it's  about your customers it's not about the  running physical data centers in your  office or it you're offering your team  more interesting and challenging work it  is a new landscape what do you wish  you'd known beforehand about security  specifically for us that there hasn't  really been there's no beans there's  been no big surprises on the security  side we we did all of our due diligence  up front our security team and our legal  team spent a lot of time with the Google  teams understanding the nuances of cloud  what did it provide what didn't it  provide the big surprise for us has been  how quickly our software engineers  embrace the cloud and in some ways we're  having to play catch-up it's interesting  you say this because you're telling me  that you know the IT teams stay in place  security stays in place they're involved  in different work so there's some change  management involved and you see a  competitive differentiator for the  enterprise and going to cloud what was  that about I think you would look at the  macro picture  we're losing the battle breaches are  getting larger and larger threat actors  are getting more and more sophisticated  and as an industry I'm not sure we're  actually winning that battle but if you  can focus less on some of the security  layers and allow your cloud provider who  has scale who can apply automation who  can apply we hope in the future machine  learning to some of those things it  frees up security professionals to focus  on our applications security and I think  it's one of the ways that we may start  stemming the tide of losing the battle  again talks and ranae you speak to many  businesses how does security figure in  for most of them as becoming some sort  of competitive differentiator what we're  trying to manage businesses when they  talk to me they're trying to manage  reputational risk right and so what do  you end up with after a breach you have  damaged reputation right and so  everybody's trying to control that and  actually it's how they get funding these  days we don't talk about threatened  vulnerabilities anymore we talk about  the impact of the to the business its  reputation like what are we gonna be  labeled you know another company who  can't seem to keep their data together  right will there be a massive fine well  and will there be a massive fine yeah  that's not even funny anymore so you  know we can't ignore it so I think from  a differentiated perspective that if you  are spending your time like Ben said  working on other things securing your  application you know making better code  having security deployed somewhere else  so that they can be meaningful I think  that's all part of being you know a  differentiator a competitive  differentiator and of course you know  the customers are also thinking how's  this going to change my life and so for  a security engineer  when you talk about cloud security and  you know how pervasive or full-stack or  you know depth of hardware software  security integration whatever what are  they thinking about what they're gonna  do next if that part of their job goes  away yeah see that's it we spend a lot  of time telling them we need to figure  out a way to get you out of the  day-to-day stuff of security looking at  patching you know looking at incidences  I'm trying to figure out if the threat  and vulnerability management system is  giving us false positives so for me it's  all about managing that third party  having a partnership understanding who's  responsible for what negotiating that  ahead of time right in a breach who  notifies who of what in a security  incident how is how are we going to  communicate that because you may be  communicating to me but I need to  communicate to an entire organization so  we need to be in sync with that we need  to negotiate change management we need  to negotiate incident management if I'm  in a heavily regulated industry and  you're making infrastructure changes I  have to know so transparency is  available exploit it use it as much  lutely do it and if you're in a heavily  regulated industry have one conversation  with the regulators before you go  down this road to understand their  expectations and if you have internal  audit have them involved the landscape  has changed and I think the next  generation of IT professionals security  professionals need to be cloud ready  they need to be less thinking about how  do I secure a Rooter am more thinking  about how do I secure my cloud account  how do I secure my keys and continue on  that journey so I think it's a journey  up the stack for all of us and when I  started in the industry we used to argue  about what physical network card to put  in a server yeah we did it was an  argument it took you a month of time to  discuss what version of card you would  put in and we're constantly moving up  the stack and it's it's only gonna  continue and it's gonna move faster and  faster and then what do you see your  eyes security teams working on a year or  two from now so what we're trying to  assess is how far can we go down the  route of service architectures not  worrying about infrastructure continuing  to abstract further and further away  from the underlying infrastructure we're  not sure it's quite there yet but we  know it's coming very very quickly we  want to be ready to embrace more  integrated and automated stack basically  basically yeah I'm bracing things like  cloud functions and embracing those  higher value services like Renee said it  like while worried about securing the  container worried about securing the  code worry about writing secure code so  for us it's it's a continual abstraction  it's never gonna stop and it's only  gonna get faster what is the future hold  for security what do you see happening  next when you think businesses should be  thinking about well I think businesses  should be thinking about getting  comfortable with the idea that you don't  need to manage this yourself I think we  should see this as fully outsourced from  a security perspective between the  managed security service providers or  cloud providers I think we should we as  organizations should get out of that  business we shouldn't be in the business  of securing data we should have  companies that are very good at securing  data we should share that risk with them  right we should do that because you  hired all the best people that I can't  afford right you hired all the best  people that I got a point board so I may  as well have you manage that for me  listen if I'm in the business of doing  research should I be in the business of  you know  data center again no no I shouldn't be  right other people are better at it  talk to the provider get a roadmap for  what's gonna get automated next and  think about the value-added security  layer you might put on top of that it's  great advice

